s (city driving) + road surface irregularities (bumps) = lots of little accelerations, often - **Planes**: rare accelerations (mostly just takeoff/landing) + occasional turbulence = long stretches of true constant velocity So it's less "cars follow different physics" and more **"cars typically experience acceleration events far more often, both from traffic patterns and from road texture."** It's a difference of *frequency and context*, not a fundamentally different set of physical laws. Your teacher wasn't *wrong* that bumps/friction cause real sensations—just maybe oversimplified it as a categorical difference rather than showing it's the same physics, just applied more chaotically on the ground.
